# Normalize line endings repo-wide: git stores LF in the index for all text;
# the Windows working tree gets CRLF via core.autocrlf.
# Added 2026-07-12 to end perpetual phantom-modified flags from pre-existing CRLF blobs.
#
# VERIFIED WORKING 2026-07-24: 617 tracked files, only genuinely-edited files show
# as modified despite a mixed LF/CRLF working tree (tools here write LF, checkout
# writes CRLF). Diffs are therefore already EOL-immune -- `git diff --ignore-cr-at-eol`
# on the 2026-07-24 backlog confirmed every reported change was real content.
#
# DELIBERATELY NOT setting a repo-wide `eol=lf`. It would not silence the cosmetic
# "LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it" notices unless every
# file's line endings were also rewritten in the working tree -- and that mass
# rewrite churns mtimes across the newest-wins guard mirrors
# (ora-task-integrity-guard/guard.ps1 compares canonical vs mirror mtimes), risking
# spurious mirror shuffling for zero diff benefit. The notices are noise, not drift.
* text=auto

# Windows script hosts are line-ending sensitive. These MUST land as CRLF on
# checkout regardless of core.autocrlf or a future clone's local settings --
# batch labels/goto and multi-line blocks can misbehave with bare LF.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.vbs text eol=crlf

# POSIX shell must stay LF -- CRLF breaks the shebang under bash/WSL.
*.sh text eol=lf
